<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">A simple weekly routine</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You do not need to post every day. Three good posts a week, every week, beats a burst of ten followed by a month of silence. A routine that works for most small businesses:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Monday:</strong> a helpful post.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> a proof post.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Friday:</strong> a personal post, with an offer in its place every other week.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Plan a month at a time. Sit down once, choose twelve ideas from the list above and note which photos you need. Then take those photos during normal working days. Most platforms let you schedule posts in advance for free through their business tools.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Which platforms to use</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Go where your customers are. For most local businesses that means Facebook and Instagram. LinkedIn suits businesses that sell to other businesses. TikTok suits visual trades and younger audiences. It is better to look after one or two pages well than to spread yourself across five.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What to avoid</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Buying followers or likes. They are not real customers and can get your account restricted.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Posting only offers.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Stock photos when you could use your own.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Ignoring comments and messages. Reply within a day.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Arguing with unhappy customers in public. Reply once, politely, and move it to a private message.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-faq","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-faq"><!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How often should a small business post?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Two or three times a week is enough for most. Being consistent matters more than how often.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>What time should I post?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When your customers are online, which is often early morning, lunchtime or evening. Each platform's business tools show when your own followers are active.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Do hashtags still matter?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A few relevant ones help people find a post, especially local ones such as your town and trade. Long lists of generic hashtags do not.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Should I use the same post on every platform?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You can reuse the idea, but adjust the size and wording. What reads well on LinkedIn is usually too formal for Instagram.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

